<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zeno of Elea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 490–c. 430 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zeno of Elea was a Greek philosopher of the Eleatic school and a pupil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parmenides">Parmenides</a>. He is famous for his paradoxes, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a> and the tortoise and the flying arrow, which argued that motion and plurality lead to contradictions and so defended Parmenides' view that reality is one and unchanging. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> called him the inventor of dialectic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zeno-of-elea.jpg" alt="Zeno of Elea" width="320" height="461" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan de Bisschop · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portretbuste_van_Zeno_van_Elea_Paradigmata_graphices_variorum_artificum_(serietitel),_RP-P-1907-4495.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zeno came from Elea, in what is now southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and little else about his life is known for certain. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> portrays him in the dialogue <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parmenides">Parmenides</a>, set when Zeno is about 40 years old, as having once been a zealous defender of his teacher, wishing to show that belief in the physical world as it appears is more absurd than belief in the Eleatic single entity of existence. How accurate the portrayal is remains unknown, though it is agreed to bear some truth.</p><p>Zeno is known to have written one book, most likely in the 460s BCE, which in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>'s account he wrote in his youth and which was stolen and published without his permission. It is lost, and his thought is known through Plato, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, Diogenes Laertius and Simplicius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Zeno was one of three major Eleatic philosophers, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parmenides">Parmenides</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melissus-of-samos">Melissus of Samos</a>. Where Melissus built on Parmenides, Zeno argued against opposing views, particularly the pluralism of the Pythagoreans. He was the first philosopher to use argumentative rather than descriptive language, writing paradoxes in the form of reductio ad absurdum. According to Proclus he had forty arguments against plurality.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> recorded four main paradoxes of motion in his Physics. The dichotomy holds that to cross a distance one must first cross half of it, and so on without end. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a> and the tortoise holds that a swift runner can never catch a slow one who has a start. The arrow holds that an arrow in flight is stationary at any given instant.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Zeno died about 430 BCE. According to Diogenes Laertius he was killed while plotting to overthrow the tyrant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nearchus">Nearchus</a>, and refused to name his fellow conspirators. He is said to have asked to whisper the names into Nearchus's ear, and to have bitten it and held on until he was killed.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> looked down on arguing from contradictions and thought Zeno did not take his arguments seriously, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> disagreed and answered the dichotomy with a distinction between actual and potential infinity. The Greek Atomists proposed the atom as a stopping point of division. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertrand-russell">Bertrand Russell</a> credited the paradoxes with making possible the work of Karl Weierstrass.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zeno of Elea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It is best known for philosophy: Parmenides, said to have written the city's laws, and his pupil <b>Zeno</b> founded the Eleatic school, which shaped Greek thinking about being and change.</blockquote><a href="/state/elea">Elea</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Velia, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Velia, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.16,15.15&amp;year=-451" aria-label="Velia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4923 727 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Zeno of Elea"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5373 997h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5373 997h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.16,15.15&amp;year=-451">What was here in 451 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
